  "chess_conclusions": [
    "At the reached late g4 state Black still has the pawn move f5. The noPawnMovesLeft mechanism from the older four-king-only state is inapplicable.",
    "All five Black roots have a winning White assessment in the final depth24 research profile. The four king retreats each permit Kf5, and each of the six legal White roots there has a winning assessment: Kf5 is useful but nonunique.",
    "After f5, exf5+ returns mate13 and a fully legal 25-ply mating suffix. Seven of eight legal White roots have a winning assessment while e5 has value0. A fixed king-entry or any-legal-pawn-push explanation would be wrong.",
    "The late sampled route starts with Ke3 and contains bxc3/bxc3. It is neither the book b3 route nor a proof that all original root responses must reach this descendant.",
    "Completed original-root comparisons support Ke3 above earlyg4 at both depths20/24, but do not by themselves prove the reserve-tempo/late-entry feature causes that root numerical gap."
  ],
